995 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 478 0 R
/E (In the robot's first tests \(V1\), we found that the main problem was not a lack of power but a lack of control over ball flow. Fuel entered the shooter misaligned, causing frequent jams and inconsistent trajectories.)
/Pg 55 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
996 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 478 0 R
/E (We also saw balls building up on the intake base, interrupting the flow to the shooter. In the code, the upper intake and feeder shafts were spinning in opposite directions, directly hurting system synchronization. Finally, our analysis of the shots showed that wheel compression was too low, reducing both speed and consistency.)
/Pg 55 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
997 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 478 0 R
/E (Based on these results, in the second iteration \(V2\) we redesigned internal fuel handling instead of simply adding shooter power. We added cone wheels to the upper intake and feeder to force the balls to the center, significantly reducing shooter entry errors.)
/Pg 55 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
998 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 478 0 R
/E (We also redistributed the compliant wheels, combining soft \(white\) and firm \(black\) wheels for progressive compression. This transferred more energy to the ball without causing blockages.)
/Pg 55 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
999 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 478 0 R
/E (In parallel, we added a stop on the intake base made from chassis material to prevent buildup and keep the flow continuous. Finally, we corrected the code to synchronize the feeder shafts, so every ball moved in the same direction.)
/Pg 55 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
1000 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 478 0 R
/E (43)
/Pg 55 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
1001 0 obj
