1030 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 483 0 R
/E (Throughout the robot's development we faced challenges that forced us to make engineering decisions based on real testing, not assumptions.)
/Pg 60 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
1031 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 483 0 R
/E (One of the most important was achieving controlled ball flow inside the robot. In the first iteration \(V1\), fuel reached the shooter misaligned, causing jams and inconsistent shots. We chose to prioritize flow control over more power, which led us to redesign the internals with cone wheels and a better compliant-wheel layout.)
/Pg 60 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
1032 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 483 0 R
/E (Mechanically, assembling the fuel mechanism plates was a critical challenge. These plates had to keep several shafts \(intake, feeder and shooter\) aligned, so any measurement error affected the whole system. This forced us to iterate the design \(V2\) and adjust spacers and supports until the alignment worked.)
/Pg 60 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
1033 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 483 0 R
/E (We also faced material limits, such as PVC spacers that did not match the required dimensions. Instead of replacing them entirely, we adjusted and adapted them, prioritizing functional solutions with the resources we had.)
/Pg 60 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
1034 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 483 0 R
/E (48)
/Pg 60 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
1035 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /Figure
/P 484 0 R
/Pg 61 0 R
/A [1943 0 R 0]
/K [0]
>>
endobj
1036 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 484 0 R
/E (Throughout the process we learned that designing a robot is not just building mechanisms, but making decisions with intent.)
/Pg 61 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
1037 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 484 0 R
/E (One of our most important lessons was that every component needs a clear purpose in the system. It was never about adding parts, but about understanding how each decision affected flow, speed and consistency. The cone wheels, for example, were not just a mechanical tweak; they were a deliberate decision to control fuel alignment.)
/Pg 61 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
1038 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 484 0 R
/E (We also learned the value of iteration. Instead of chasing a perfect solution from the start, we worked in versions \(V1, V2, V3\), and every test gave us data to improve. This took us from an unstable system to a consistent, competitive one.)
/Pg 61 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
1039 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 484 0 R
/E (Another key lesson was the importance of prototyping. Simple models, like the cardboard prototype of the extendable hopper, let us validate ideas before investing time and resources in the final build.)
/Pg 61 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
1040 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 484 0 R
/E (Finally, we learned that testing is a central part of design. Many of our most important decisions came from watching the robot fail in real tests.)
/Pg 61 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
1041 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /Figure
/P 484 0 R
/Pg 61 0 R
/A [1944 0 R 0]
/K [6]
>>
endobj
1042 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 484 0 R
/E (In electronics and programming, we found errors that directly hurt performance, such as the reversed rotation of the upper intake and feeder shafts. This reinforced the importance of validating not only the physical design but also the system's logic.)
/Pg 61 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
